ubscribe?&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Subscribe now&quot;,&quot;action&quot;:null,&quot;class&quot;:null}\" data-component-name=\"ButtonCreateButton\"><a class=\"button primary\" href=\"https://thefrontierpsychiatrists.substack.com/subscribe?\"><span>Subscribe now</span></a></p><p></p>","text":"With great domain rating comes great responsibility. I'm pretty sure that's what they said in Spider-Man. And, after all, Peter Parker was a journalist. Maybe that's the great power they were talking about?\n\nNewspapers had distribution. Television shows have Nielsen ratings. With the Internet, lets us do something more detailed. We've got not only the number of people who come to your website, but how authoritative other people think it is.\n\nDomain Rating is a measure, created by Ahrefs, to measure the following:\n\n“Website authority” is an SEO concept that refers to the “strength” of a given domain.\n\nSome people call this “domain authority,” which is not to be confused with the Domain Authority (DA) metric from Moz. When we talk about domain authority, we’re talking about a general SEO concept that’s synonymous with “website authority.”\n\nHere at Ahrefs, we have a website authority metric of our own called Domain Rating. It runs on a scale from zero to a hundred. The higher a website’s Domain Rating (DR), the stronger and more authoritative it is.\n\nThey lay out how they do the math to get to their domain rating for any given site:\n\nDomain Rating (DR) looks at the quantity and quality of external backlinks to a website.\n\n• Look at how many unique domains link to the target website;\n\n• Look at the “authority” of those linking domains;\n\n• Take into account how many unique domains each of those sites link to;\n\n• Apply some math and coding magic to calculate “raw” DR scores;\n\n• Plot these scores on a 100-point scale.\n\nDomain Rating doesn’t take into account any other variables like link spam, traffic, domain age, etc.\n\nLearn more about Domain Rating here .\n\nThat hundred point scale is logarithmic. That is to say, it's not equidistant between any two numbers, and it gets harder and harder to have your domain rating get higher and higher. Naturally, I checked my own website, The Frontier Psychiatrists:\n\nThat seems…good.\n\nHow does it compare to a legit trade publication in psychiatry?\n\nBetter. Gulp. Ok, that is a one off, I’m sure.\n\nI’ll check the homepage of a peer reviewed journal, like The Journal of Clinical Psychiatry (JCP) :\n\nWell, I guess it's better again. But psychiatrist.com is just a really good domain name. I'm sure if I look at the most institutional source of knowledge on the Internet about psychiatry, the American psychiatric Association own website for publishing content from the DSM-5-TR, the American Journal of Psychiatry, and associated resources, it would definitely rank higher than my newsletter?\n\nBut it does…not. This is awkward.\n\nHumans of the Internet, this newsletter has arrived. It may not have the most traffic, it may not be completely serious all the time, but it has the Internet compared to most things I could think to check. I'm not saying this newsletter Won the Internet come up, but I've got a higher rating than NEJM:\n\nI’m confused. At least the NY Times homepage beats me…by one point.\n\nThe Internet is weird. But consistency does pay off. Thanks for reading, feel free to share your thoughts in the comments. I could just be doing the search wrong?\n\nSee you all in the coming week!\n\nAddendum: that math is based on SUBSTACK, not just me. Trombones getting sad:\n\nShare\n\nSubscribe now"}